Here's a graph of a normal distribution with the Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7) illustrated:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Axes
    \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {$f(x)$};

    % Normal distribution curve
    \draw[blue, thick, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {exp(-(\x)^2/2)});

    % Standard deviation markers
    \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {
        \draw[thick] (\x,0.01) -- (\x,-0.01) node[below] {$\x\sigma$};
    }

    % Empirical Rule labels
    \draw[<->, thick] (-3,0.01) -- (-2,0.01) node[midway, above] {13.5\%};
    \draw[<->, thick] (-2,0.01) -- (-1,0.01) node[midway, above] {13.5\%};
    \draw[<->, thick] (-1,0.01) -- (0,0.01) node[midway, above] {34\%};
    \draw[<->, thick] (0,0.01) -- (1,0.01) node[midway, above] {34\%};
    \draw[<->, thick] (1,0.01) -- (2,0.01) node[midway, above] {13.5\%};
    \draw[<->, thick] (2,0.01) -- (3,0.01) node[midway, above] {13.5\%};

    % Total percentages
    \node at (0,0.8) {68\%};
    \node at (0,1.0) {95\%};
    \node at (0,1.1) {99.7\%};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7) states that for a normal distribution:

1. [h]68%[/h] of the data falls within [c]1 standard deviation[/c] of the mean (between $-1\sigma$ and $1\sigma$)
2. [h]95%[/h] of the data falls within [c]2 standard deviations[/c] of the mean (between $-2\sigma$ and $2\sigma$)
3. [h]99.7%[/h] of the data falls within [c]3 standard deviations[/c] of the mean (between $-3\sigma$ and $3\sigma$)

This rule is a quick way to estimate the spread of data in a normal distribution. The percentages represent the proportion of data that falls within each range from the mean.

Here's a Venn diagram showing the relationship between squares and rectangles:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Define the circles
    \coordinate (rectangle) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (square) at (1.5,0);

    % Draw the circles
    \draw[thick] (rectangle) circle (2cm);
    \draw[thick] (square) circle (1.2cm);

    % Label the circles
    \node at (rectangle) {Rectangles};
    \node at (square) {Squares};

    % Add the intersection label
    \node at (0.75,0) {All Squares are Rectangles};

    % Add the unique parts
    \node at (-1.5,0) {Other Rectangles};
    \node at (2.25,0) {Only Squares};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This Venn diagram shows that:

1. [h]All squares are rectangles[/h] (the overlapping area)
2. [h]Not all rectangles are squares[/h] (the non-overlapping part of the rectangle circle)
3. [h]Squares have all the properties of rectangles[/h] (equal sides and right angles) plus the additional property that all sides are equal

This relationship is important in geometry because it shows how specific shapes can be subsets of more general shape categories.

Here's an area model for multiplying $(x + 2)$ and $(x + 5)$:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Draw the outer rectangle
    \draw[very thick] (0,0) rectangle (5,3);

    % Divide the rectangle into four parts
    \draw[thick] (2,0) -- (2,3);
    \draw[thick] (0,1) -- (5,1);

    % Label the sides
    \node at (1,3.2) {$x$};
    \node at (3.5,3.2) {$5$};
    \node at (-0.3,2) {$x$};
    \node at (-0.3,0.5) {$2$};

    % Label the products
    \node at (1,1.5) {$x^2$};
    \node at (3.5,1.5) {$5x$};
    \node at (1,0.5) {$2x$};
    \node at (3.5,0.5) {$10$};

    % Label the final expression
    \node at (2.5,3.5) {$(x + 2)(x + 5) = x^2 + 7x + 10$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This area model shows how to multiply two binomials by:

1. Drawing a rectangle and dividing it into four parts
2. Labeling the sides with the terms from each binomial
3. Calculating the product for each section of the rectangle
4. Adding all the products together to get the final expression

The area model helps visualize the distributive property in polynomial multiplication. Each section of the rectangle represents one of the products in the FOIL method (First, Outer, Inner, Last).

Here's a complex Venn diagram showing the relationships between squares, rectangles, and parallelograms:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.4, transform shape]
    % Define the circles
    \coordinate (parallelogram) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (rectangle) at (1.5,0);
    \coordinate (square) at (0.75,1.5);

    % Draw the circles
    \draw[thick] (parallelogram) circle (2.5cm);
    \draw[thick] (rectangle) circle (1.8cm);
    \draw[thick] (square) circle (1.2cm);

    % Label the circles
    \node at (parallelogram) {Parallelograms};
    \node at (rectangle) {Rectangles};
    \node at (square) {Squares};

    % Add the intersection labels
    \node at (0.75,0) {All Rectangles are Parallelograms};
    \node at (0.75,1.2) {All Squares are Rectangles};
    \node at (0.75,0.6) {All Squares are Parallelograms};

    % Add the unique parts
    \node at (-1.5,0) {Other Parallelograms};
    \node at (2.25,0) {Other Rectangles};
    \node at (0.75,2.2) {Only Squares};

    % Add the relationships
    \draw[<->, thick] (0.75,0.3) -- (0.75,0.9) node[midway, right] {Subset};
    \draw[<->, thick] (0.75,0.9) -- (0.75,1.5) node[midway, right] {Subset};
    \draw[<->, thick] (0.75,0.3) -- (1.5,0.3) node[midway, above] {Subset};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This Venn diagram shows that:

1. [h]All squares are rectangles[/h] (the intersection of all three circles)
2. [h]All rectangles are parallelograms[/h] (the intersection of the rectangle and parallelogram circles)
3. [h]Not all parallelograms are rectangles[/h] (the non-overlapping part of the parallelogram circle)
4. [h]Not all rectangles are squares[/h] (the non-overlapping part of the rectangle circle)
5. [h]Squares have all the properties of rectangles and parallelograms[/h] (equal sides, right angles, and opposite sides parallel and equal)

This relationship is important in geometry because it shows how specific shapes can be subsets of more general shape categories, with each category having progressively more specific properties.

Here's a normal distribution curve with the area between -1 and 1 standard deviations shaded in light blue:

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2, transform shape]
    % Axes
    \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
    \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {$f(x)$};

    % Normal distribution curve
    \draw[blue, thick, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {exp(-(\x)^2/2)});

    % Standard deviation markers
    \foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {
        \draw[thick] (\x,0.01) -- (\x,-0.01) node[below] {$\x\sigma$};
    }

    % Shade the area between -1 and 1 standard deviations
    \fill[blue!20] (-1,0) -- (-1,{exp(-(-1)^2/2)}) -- plot[domain=-1:1, samples=100] (\x, {exp(-(\x)^2/2)}) -- (1,{exp(-(1)^2/2)}) -- (1,0) -- cycle;

    % Add labels
    \node at (0,0.8) {68\%};
    \node at (0,0.5) {of data falls};
    \node at (0,0.2) {within 1σ};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This diagram shows:

1. [h]The normal distribution curve[/h] (bell-shaped curve)
2. [h]Standard deviation markers[/h] at -3σ, -2σ, -1σ, 1σ, 2σ, and 3σ
3. [h]The shaded area[/h] between -1σ and 1σ, representing 68% of the data

The shading highlights the area that contains 68% of the data in a normal distribution, according to the Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7). This is a key concept in statistics for understanding how data is distributed around the mean in a normal distribution.
